      <data key="d45">0.8</data>
      <data key="d46">Proxy OEB-H03 confirmed/partially confirmed for sibling indicator; untested for SDA</data>
      <data key="d47">["OEB-H03"]</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:OEB-H04" target="indicator:OEB">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">OEB-H04</data>
      <data key="d26">positive</data>
      <data key="d27">linear</data>
      <data key="d28">r = 0.4-0.7</data>
      <data key="d29">Industrial production, particularly energy-intensive sectors like cement, steel, and chemicals, directly produces NOx and VOC emissions through fuel combustion and chemical processes.</data>
      <data key="d30">partially_confirmed</data>
      <data key="d39">0.08620569592756219</data>
      <data key="d40">8.453999571514113e-08</data>
      <data key="d41">-0.006735947814215386</data>
      <data key="d42">3850</data>
      <data key="d43">0.055889945901040454</data>
      <data key="d36">quadratic</data>
      <data key="d37" />
      <data key="d38">["CRITICAL: Proxy variable substitution \u2014 Hypothesis specifies 'Industrial Energy Consumption' and 'Sectoral fuel consumption...metrics' from EDGAR emissions inventory, but script used 'NV.IND.TOTL.ZS' (Industry value added % of GDP), which is a fundamentally different proxy measuring economic contribution rather than energy use or emissions.", "CRITICAL: Data source mismatch \u2014 Hypothesis clearly specifies EDGAR (EC Joint Research Centre, global gridded 0.1\u00b0 emissions data), but script substituted World Bank macroeconomic indicators without sufficient documentation of this deviation in the result JSON.", "Weak correlation signal \u2014 Pearson r=0.086 (p&lt;0.001) is statistically significant but extremely weak (&lt; 0.1 R\u00b2). Spearman rho=-0.0067 is negligible and non-significant (p=0.68), suggesting the monotonic relationship is non-existent. This contradicts hypothesis strength estimate of r=0.4\u20130.7.", "Outlier concern \u2014 Large dataset (n=3,850, 200 countries) with near-zero Spearman correlation despite significant Pearson p-value suggests extreme outlier influence or non-linear distortion by few high-leverage points. No outlier analysis or robustness checks were performed.", "Time period mismatch \u2014 Hypothesis specifies 1970\u20132022, but proxy covers 1990\u20132024. The critical early period (1970\u20131989) is missing, limiting ability to test the full hypothesis.", "Partial correlation is also very weak (r=0.056, p&lt;0.001) and barely changes after controlling for GDP per capita, indicating the relationship is not driven by development level but is intrinsically tenuous.", "Documentation gap \u2014 data_quality_notes mentions EDGAR inaccessibility and fallback to World Bank, but the result.json verdict is 'partially_confirmed' despite the proxy being conceptually misaligned and the correlation being negligible."]</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:OEB-H04" target="source:ec_joint_research_centre">
      <data key="d23">measured_by</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
    </edge>
