    <edge source="proxy:OEB-H07" target="indicator:OEB">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">OEB-H07</data>
      <data key="d26">positive</data>
      <data key="d27">threshold</data>
      <data key="d28">r = 0.4-0.6</data>
      <data key="d29">Solar radiation intensity directly drives photochemical reactions that produce ozone from NOx and VOCs. Higher radiation increases ozone formation rates up to a saturation point.</data>
      <data key="d30">rejected</data>
      <data key="d39">-0.27620619835071714</data>
      <data key="d40">6.769219365707973e-60</data>
      <data key="d41">-0.5076004249668159</data>
      <data key="d42">3360</data>
      <data key="d43">-0.12324259952659977</data>
      <data key="d36">quadratic</data>
      <data key="d37" />
      <data key="d38">["SYNTHETIC DATA: The fallback proxy (SW \u2248 200 + 200*cos(|lat|) W/m\u00b2) is a hardcoded mathematical formula, not real measurements. It assigns the same irradiance value to every year for each country, making it deterministic rather than observational.", "DATA SOURCE MISMATCH: The hypothesis specifies NASA CERES satellite data in NetCDF format; the script attempted NASA POWER API instead (acknowledged in data_quality_notes as fallback), but ultimately used a latitude-based formula when the API failed. This is a substantial substitution not documented in the hypothesis.", "HYPOTHESIS INTERPRETATION PROBLEM: The proxy should measure annual variation in solar radiation intensity (cloud-free days or direct normal irradiance with year-to-year variation). The latitude formula has zero time-series variance\u2014each country's 'irradiance' is identical across all 20 years. This does not test the hypothesis mechanism (radiation-driven photochemistry) but rather the geographic latitude\u2013ozone relationship.", "NEGATIVE CORRELATION REVERSAL: The result shows Pearson r = \u22120.276 and Spearman rho = \u22120.508, strongly negative correlations opposite to the hypothesis prediction (positive direction). With a latitude-only proxy, equatorial countries (high latitude irradiance) would be compared against high-latitude countries (low latitude irradiance). Since ozone formation depends on temperature, precursor concentration, and atmospheric mixing (all latitude-dependent), the negative correlation likely reflects confounding by latitude and season rather than solar radiation intensity per se.", "OUTLIER CONCERN: With 3,360 observations across 168 countries and a strong negative correlation from a deterministic proxy, there is high risk that a few countries or latitude bands are driving the entire signal. No outlier detection or influence diagnostics are reported in the script.", "TEMPORAL VARIANCE LOSS: By using a constant proxy value for all years within each country, the script discards all temporal variation in solar radiation (clouds, seasonal shifts, etc.). This fundamentally breaks the ability to test the hypothesis mechanism on the time scale specified (monthly, 2000\u2013present)."]</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:OEB-H07" target="source:nasa">
      <data key="d23">measured_by</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:OEB-H08" target="indicator:OEB">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">OEB-H08</data>
      <data key="d26">negative</data>
      <data key="d27">linear</data>
      <data key="d28">r = -0.3 to -0.5</data>
      <data key="d29">Ground-level ozone reduces net photosynthesis, accelerates senescence, and reduces biomass allocation to grain, thereby reducing harvestable yield in sensitive crops.</data>
      <data key="d30">rejected</data>
      <data key="d39">0.2214045896052544</data>
      <data key="d40">2.0316819914767055e-40</data>
      <data key="d41">0.13745373886366322</data>
      <data key="d42">3527</data>
      <data key="d43">0.09568565885593391</data>
      <data key="d36">linear</data>
      <data key="d37" />
      <data key="d38">["hypothesis_interpretation_mismatch: The hypothesis specifies 'national average crop yields for ozone-sensitive crops (wheat, soybeans, rice, maize)' but the script uses World Bank AG.YLD.CREL.KG (cereal yield, kg/ha), which is a broad cereal category. Cereals include non-ozone-sensitive crops (e.g., barley, rye) and do not specifically measure wheat, soybeans, rice, or maize yields. This is a material simplification that dilutes the proxy signal.", "confounding_direction_issue: The hypothesis predicts negative correlation (high ozone \u2192 low yields), but the raw Pearson r=0.221 is positive and highly significant (p&lt;0.001). This is the opposite of the expected direction. The script labels this as 'rejected' but does not adequately discuss why the sign is reversed. Possible explanations (e.g., developed countries have both higher yields AND higher ozone from industrial sources, confounding via GPC) are mentioned but not tested rigorously.", "weak_signal_after_confounding: The partial correlation controlling for log(GPC) drops dramatically from r=0.221 to r=0.096 (56% reduction), suggesting that much of the apparent crop-yield association is driven by economic development, not ozone. This severely undermines the proxy's validity.", "temporal_scope_unstated: The result.json does not specify the year range of the merged dataset. The hypothesis requests 'annual, 1961-present,' but the actual temporal coverage is not reported, making it impossible to verify whether the data span the full hypothesis period.", "data_quality_confounding_acknowledged_but_not_addressed: The script correctly acknowledges that 'crop yields are influenced by many factors beyond ozone (fertilizers, irrigation, breeding improvements, weather)' but does not attempt to isolate the ozone signal via residualization or other methods. The confounding is recognized but left unresolved."]</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:OEB-H08" target="source:food_and_agriculture_organization">
      <data key="d23">measured_by</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:OEB-H09" target="indicator:OEB">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">OEB-H09</data>
      <data key="d26">negative</data>
      <data key="d27">linear</data>
      <data key="d28">r = -0.5 to -0.8</data>
      <data key="d29">Ozone impairs photosynthesis by causing oxidative stress, reducing leaf area and transpiration, ultimately decreasing net primary productivity and biomass accumulation.</data>
      <data key="d30">confirmed</data>
      <data key="d39">-0.4948500730854384</data>
      <data key="d40">6.094479993034927e-245</data>
      <data key="d41">-0.4888949656250192</data>
      <data key="d42">3980</data>
      <data key="d43">-0.5244348563821647</data>
      <data key="d36">quadratic</data>
      <data key="d37" />
      <data key="d38">["CRITICAL: Proxy variable substitution is materially different from hypothesis specification. Hypothesis calls for MODIS-derived NPP in g C m\u207b\u00b2 day\u207b\u00b9 (a flux measure of photosynthetic productivity); script uses World Bank forest area (% of land area), which is a stock measure of land cover. Forest area does not directly measure photosynthetic capacity or annual carbon accumulation.", "CRITICAL: Data source inauthentic. Hypothesis specifies 'MODIS NPP' from NASA; script documents using 'World Bank AG.LND.FRST.ZS (Forest area % of land area)' instead. The data_quality_notes acknowledge MODIS NPP is HDF4 and inaccessible, but this substitution was not pre-authorized and fundamentally changes what is being tested.", "MAJOR: Mechanistic implausibility. The hypothesis mechanism is 'ozone impairs photosynthesis \u2192 reduces NPP and biomass.' Testing forest area (a slow-changing stock variable) against OEB (ozone exposure in Key Biodiversity Areas) does not measure photosynthetic damage or carbon flux reduction. A country's forest area is driven primarily by deforestation/afforestation policy, not by ozone-induced productivity loss.", "MODERATE: Temporal mismatch concern. The hypothesis specifies 'monthly' temporal resolution; the actual analysis uses annual country-level data (merged OEB + forest area). This loses the fine-grained temporal information needed to detect acute ozone damage.", "MODERATE: Geographic scope mismatch. Hypothesis specifies 'global forested regions' but analysis is country-level aggregation. MODIS NPP at 1 km resolution could target forested grid cells; World Bank forest area is a country-wide percentage, averaging across regions with very different forest density and ozone exposure.", "MODERATE: The script documents a fallback to cereal yield (AG.YLD.CREL.KG) if forest area fails, which is even further from NPP and would represent agricultural productivity only, not forest ecosystem productivity."]</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:OEB-H09" target="source:nasa">
      <data key="d23">measured_by</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
    </edge>
