    <edge source="proxy:PHL-H01" target="indicator:PHL">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">PHL-H01</data>
      <data key="d26">negative</data>
      <data key="d27">log-linear</data>
      <data key="d28">near-perfect inverse theoretical alignment</data>
      <data key="d29">HFP explicitly utilizes 'croplands' and 'built environments' as two of its primary weighted input layers, so an increase in HFP within a protected area mathematically guarantees a decrease in PHL</data>
      <data key="d30">partially_confirmed</data>
      <data key="d39">-0.30590217986718193</data>
      <data key="d40">4.045229421222908e-05</data>
      <data key="d41">-0.4427176172442268</data>
      <data key="d42">174</data>
      <data key="d35" />
      <data key="d36">quadratic</data>
      <data key="d48">8</data>
      <data key="d38">["Material proxy substitution: The hypothesis specifies 'Global Human Footprint Index' (Mu et al. 2022, full composite of 8 variables), but the verification used only 'World Bank Agricultural Land' (croplands only), which is a substantially different variable. This is not a minor data availability adjustment but a conceptually different proxy.", "Data source not authentic: The hypothesis explicitly names 'Multiple iterations, prominently Mu et al. (2022)' from figshare GeoTIFF as the data source. The verification script explicitly acknowledges that HFP raster data cannot be obtained as country-level aggregates via APIs, then substitutes an entirely different World Bank indicator without attempting alternative HFP data sources (e.g., direct download and spatial aggregation, alternative HFP implementations, or recent global 100m datasets).", "Hypothesis interpretation problem: The causal mechanism depends on HFP's 'explicit utilization of croplands AND built environments as two of its primary weighted input layers' creating 'mathematical guarantee' of inverse alignment. Using only croplands breaks this mechanism\u2014the stated 'near-perfect inverse theoretical alignment' is claimed to arise from the full composite, not a single component.", "Acknowledged but unresolved: The script's own data_quality_notes admit 'This covers only one of eight HFP input layers' and that 'The near-perfect theoretical alignment claimed by Mu et al. 2022 refers to the full composite HFP dataset,' yet the verdict still marks the hypothesis as 'partially_confirmed' based on this weakened proxy. The correlation magnitude (Pearson r = -0.306, Spearman rho = -0.443) is moderate, not 'near-perfect,' raising questions about whether the partial proxy is adequate.", "Year alignment appears sound (2022 data matched across proxy and target), and the sample size (n=174 countries) is adequate, so those aspects are not flagged. However, the fundamental proxy substitution undermines the core verification claim."]</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:PHL-H01" target="source:multiple_iterations,_prominently_mu_et_al._(2022)">
      <data key="d23">measured_by</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
    </edge>
