    <edge source="proxy:SPI-H02" target="indicator:SPI">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">SPI-H02</data>
      <data key="d26">positive</data>
      <data key="d27">threshold</data>
      <data key="d28">strong (estimated r&gt;0.65)</data>
      <data key="d29">LBII measures local species persistence and ecosystem intactness, outcomes that correlate strongly with protected area effectiveness. Countries with high land-use pressure and low local species intactness would show moderate-to-low SPI if protected areas are scattered through heavily used landscapes.</data>
      <data key="d30">rejected</data>
      <data key="d39">-0.14461848822300974</data>
      <data key="d40">0.06984188783094727</data>
      <data key="d41">-0.21363337017861075</data>
      <data key="d42">158</data>
      <data key="d43">-0.07253682246779683</data>
      <data key="d36">linear</data>
      <data key="d37" />
      <data key="d38">["CRITICAL: Proxy substitution is conceptually invalid. LBII measures biodiversity persistence under land-use pressure; EN.CLC.MDAT.ZS measures the percentage of important biodiversity sites that are protected. These are not equivalent. EN.CLC.MDAT.ZS is itself a protection metric (similar to what SPI measures), creating circular reasoning rather than testing a causal mechanism.", "CRITICAL: True LBII was never accessed. The hypothesis explicitly specifies LBII from GEO BON/UN Biodiversity Lab (raster/GeoTIFF, 1km resolution, 2001-present). The script attempted only World Bank indicators after failing to download the original source, then settled on EN.CLC.MDAT.ZS without documenting this fundamental substitution in mechanistic terms.", "CRITICAL: Year alignment issue. The script uses the 'latest year per country' for both proxy and target (cross-sectional snapshot), collapsing all temporal variation. The hypothesis specifies a time period of '2001-present,' but the verification discards the temporal dimension entirely, making it impossible to test the hypothesis as stated.", "Year alignment detail: By selecting only the most recent non-null observation per country for both variables, the script does not ensure proxy and target years match. If SPI's most recent year is 2020 for country A and the EN.CLC.MDAT.ZS most recent year is 2015, they are merged without temporal alignment, introducing a systematic bias.", "Data source mismatch not transparently flagged. The data_quality_notes acknowledge that 'True LBII (raster product at 1km resolution) was not accessible as country-level tabular data,' but this is buried. The mechanism section should have stated upfront that EN.CLC.MDAT.ZS is a poor proxy because it measures protection, not intactness.", "Negative correlation result (-r=0.145, p=0.07) contradicts the hypothesis's predicted positive direction, but this outcome is largely an artifact of the proxy substitution rather than evidence against the true LBII mechanism.", "No attempt to aggregate LBII raster data to country level. The script could have downloaded the GeoTIFF, computed country-level mean intactness values, and merged with SPI. Instead, it gave up and used an unrelated World Bank indicator."]</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:SPI-H02" target="source:geo_bon,_un_biodiversity_lab">
      <data key="d23">measured_by</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:SPI-H03" target="indicator:SPI">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">SPI-H03</data>
      <data key="d26">positive</data>
      <data key="d27">linear</data>
      <data key="d28">r&gt;0.70</data>
      <data key="d29">SHI focuses on the extent and integrity of remaining suitable habitat (the denominator in SPI calculations), while SPI measures the proportion of that habitat within protected areas. Countries with high SHI have greater opportunity to achieve high SPI through strategic protected area placement.</data>
      <data key="d30">rejected</data>
      <data key="d39">-0.1472801110648606</data>
      <data key="d40">9.77436690040775e-19</data>
      <data key="d41">-0.22689576832114122</data>
      <data key="d42">3564</data>
      <data key="d43">-0.14574914194704372</data>
      <data key="d36">quadratic</data>
      <data key="d37" />
      <data key="d38">["Hypothesis mechanism appears theoretically flawed based on empirical results: the negative correlation contradicts the assumed positive relationship, suggesting SHI and SPI measure fundamentally different policy outcomes rather than opportunity-constraint pairs.", "The partial correlation controlling for log GDP/capita remains significantly negative (r=-0.146, p&lt;0.001), indicating the relationship is not driven by economic confounding and reflects a genuine inverse pattern.", "Very weak explained variance across all functional forms (best R\u00b2=0.026), suggesting SHI is a poor predictor of SPI even where correlation is significant. The quadratic form shows marginally better fit but still explains &lt;3% of variance.", "No investigation of potential reverse causality: countries prioritizing protected areas might reduce habitat outside reserves, or alternatively, countries with vast intact areas may have lower pressure to formalize protection (leakage effect).", "The hypothesis cites a literature expectation of r&gt;0.70 (Jetz et al. 2019), but the observed r=-0.147 is not only below this but opposite in sign. The script does not investigate whether this citation actually supports the positive relationship or if there is a mischaracterization of the literature."]</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:SPI-H03" target="source:map_of_life,_environmental_performance_index">
      <data key="d23">measured_by</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:SPI-H04" target="indicator:SPI">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">SPI-H04</data>
      <data key="d26">positive</data>
      <data key="d27">linear</data>
      <data key="d28">strong with lag effect</data>
      <data key="d29">Countries achieving high SPI values should show improving or stable RLI values, as representative protected area networks reduce extinction risk. Species protected in recent years may require 5-10 years to demonstrate population recovery.</data>
      <data key="d30">partially_confirmed</data>
      <data key="d39">0.15076853800184742</data>
      <data key="d40">5.428337278266222e-30</data>
      <data key="d41">0.11211649943436792</data>
      <data key="d42">5632</data>
      <data key="d43">0.14699120004063507</data>
      <data key="d36">log-linear</data>
      <data key="d37" />
      <data key="d38">["Lag effect hypothesis not tested: The hypothesis explicitly posits a 5-10 year lag effect between protected area establishment and species recovery detection, but the verify.py script performs only contemporaneous (same-year) matching. The script acknowledges this limitation in data_quality_notes but does not actually implement a lagged analysis, failing to test the core mechanism.", "Weak correlation magnitude: The observed Pearson r=0.151 and Spearman rho=0.112 are statistically significant but very weak. With R\u00b2\u22480.023, RLI explains less than 2.3% of SPI variance. While the verdict is 'partially_confirmed', this correlation is far below typical expectations for a 'strong' relationship as claimed in the hypothesis.", "Proxy interpretation concern: The RLI is a global aggregate measure of extinction risk across all assessed species (biased toward vertebrates), while SPI measures a country's protected area policy performance. These measure fundamentally different constructs\u2014one is an outcome (species status), one is a policy input (protection effort). The weak correlation may reflect this conceptual mismatch rather than genuine policy ineffectiveness."]</data>
    </edge>
