    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:SPI-H07" target="indicator:SPI">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">SPI-H07</data>
      <data key="d26">positive</data>
      <data key="d27">threshold</data>
      <data key="d28">r=0.60-0.75 (estimated)</data>
      <data key="d29">Countries achieving both high protected area coverage AND high average PAME scores show higher SPI because protected areas actually conserve habitat and support species populations rather than existing as 'paper parks'. Management effectiveness directly determines whether habitat degradation inside protected areas constrains species persistence.</data>
      <data key="d30">partially_confirmed</data>
      <data key="d39">0.0625102667896816</data>
      <data key="d40">3.49619291765642e-05</data>
      <data key="d41">0.03342539649206063</data>
      <data key="d42">4377</data>
      <data key="d43">0.011166425593540174</data>
      <data key="d36">quadratic</data>
      <data key="d37" />
      <data key="d38">["Major: Proxy substitution is materially different from hypothesis intent. GD-PAME/METT-4 measures park-level management effectiveness (legal designation, planning, staffing, enforcement, stakeholder engagement). Government Effectiveness (WGI) is a country-level governance index covering broader institutional quality. This is not a reasonable substitution\u2014it tests governance-SPI correlation, not management-effectiveness-SPI correlation.", "Major: Data source authentication failed. The hypothesis specifies GD-PAME from UNEP-WCMC/BIOPAMA. The script could not access this source and substituted with World Bank WGI without explicit documentation in the hypothesis caveat section. The proxy_substitution field acknowledges this in result.json, but this constitutes a fundamental departure from the hypothesis specification.", "Moderate: Bivariate correlation is weak (r=0.063, p&lt;0.0001) and practically non-meaningful despite statistical significance due to large N (4377 obs). After controlling for GDP per capita (partial r=0.011, p=0.47), the relationship becomes indistinguishable from zero. This suggests Government Effectiveness does not capture PAME's mechanism.", "Moderate: Functional form is quadratic but with very low R\u00b2 (0.0052), indicating the relationship explains &lt;1% of variance. The quadratic fit (AIC=29023.49) is only marginally better than linear (AIC=29027.31), suggesting no meaningful non-linear threshold effect as hypothesized.", "Minor: The script's fallback to 'year-agnostic' merge (most recent values per country) could mask temporal misalignment if Government Effectiveness and SPI data come from different years for the same country. The merged dataset combines 4377 year-country observations, but the year_alignment logic is not fully documented."]</data>
    </edge>
