    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:WRR-H08" target="indicator:WRR">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">WRR-H08</data>
      <data key="d26">positive</data>
      <data key="d27">threshold</data>
      <data key="d28">strong (r=0.45-0.60 expected)</data>
      <data key="d29">Food and garden waste comprise 45-60% of municipal waste stream and are most easily recovered via composting. Countries mandating food waste segregation directly increase WRR by forcing organic waste toward recovery pathways.</data>
      <data key="d30">confirmed</data>
      <data key="d39">0.6864358626636478</data>
      <data key="d40">1.0145085045740187e-52</data>
      <data key="d41">0.7376572003299098</data>
      <data key="d42">369</data>
      <data key="d35" />
      <data key="d36">quadratic</data>
      <data key="d37" />
      <data key="d38">["CRITICAL: Proxy variable substitution is not reasonable. The hypothesis specifies a binary/ordinal indicator of food waste legislation (segregation mandates, donation requirements, landfill bans). The script used SH.STA.SMSS.ZS (safely managed sanitation %), which measures water/sanitation access, not food waste policy. This is a materially different variable from different policy domain.", "CRITICAL: Data source not authentic. The hypothesis requires REFASH legislative database or equivalent food waste legislation tracking. The script substituted a World Bank sanitation indicator without documenting this as a forced substitution due to data unavailability. The data_quality_notes acknowledges the approximation but the verdict was still marked 'confirmed' (r=0.686), which conflates sanitation governance correlation with food waste legislation effectiveness.", "MAJOR: Hypothesis interpretation compromised. Testing sanitation services as a proxy for food waste legislation is not a reasonable substitution \u2014 it measures different infrastructure, different policy mechanisms, and different environmental outcomes. Sanitation is about human health/water safety; food waste legislation is about circular economy/waste diversion. The causal mechanisms are distinct.", "Data source mismatch documented in notes but verdict not downgraded. The script correctly identifies in data_quality_notes that 'the original hypothesis required a binary dataset of food waste segregation mandates from REFASH legislative database, which is not machine-readable' and marks it as 'approximate proxy'. However, the verdict remains 'confirmed' with strong correlation (r=0.686, p&lt;0.001) based on an unrelated indicator. This is misleading \u2014 a strong sanitation\u2013WRR correlation does not validate a food waste legislation hypothesis.", "Mechanistic implausibility obscured by strong statistical association. The correlation is strong (r=0.686) but spurious relative to the stated hypothesis. Both WRR and sanitation services are driven by general environmental governance/economic capacity, creating confounding rather than causal evidence. No partial correlation controlling for GDP was successfully run (n=369 merged observations but GPC merge failed), so the confounding cannot be assessed."]</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:WRR-H08" target="source:refash">
