    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:WRR-H09" target="indicator:WRR">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">WRR-H09</data>
      <data key="d26">positive</data>
      <data key="d27">linear</data>
      <data key="d28">moderate (r=0.35-0.50 expected)</data>
      <data key="d29">Countries with developed e-waste recycling systems show &gt;70% collection/recycling rates and typically have general waste recovery infrastructure. E-waste infrastructure requires technical sophistication that correlates with general waste management maturity.</data>
      <data key="d30">partially_confirmed</data>
      <data key="d39">0.2604243162268417</data>
      <data key="d40">5.499994313204979e-08</data>
      <data key="d41">0.20649160967251662</data>
      <data key="d42">423</data>
      <data key="d35" />
      <data key="d36">linear</data>
      <data key="d37" />
      <data key="d38">["MAJOR: Proxy variable substitution \u2014 hypothesis specifies 'e-waste recycling rate' but script uses 'renewable electricity as % of total' (World Bank EG.ELC.RNEW.ZS). These are materially different variables. Renewable energy output reflects energy policy and resource endowment, not waste management infrastructure.", "MAJOR: Data source not authentic \u2014 the hypothesis explicitly requires 'Global E-waste Monitor 2024' (ITU/UNITAR). The script could not access this source and substituted World Bank renewable electricity data without adequate justification. The data_quality_notes acknowledge this substitution but do not explain why renewable electricity is a valid proxy for e-waste recycling.", "MODERATE: Weak mechanistic link \u2014 the claimed mechanism ('countries with advanced renewable energy infrastructure tend to also have developed waste management systems') is plausible but indirect. The script provides no evidence that renewable energy infrastructure causally relates to e-waste recycling capacity. This is a confounding-by-development inference, not a direct proxy.", "MINOR: Partial correlation not computed \u2014 although 423 observations and 132 countries are available, partial correlation controlling for GDP was skipped. This would help isolate the proxy effect from general development level, which is critical given the development-driven confounding."]</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:WRR-H09" target="source:itu/unitar">
      <data key="d23">measured_by</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:WRR-H10" target="indicator:WRR">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">WRR-H10</data>
      <data key="d26">positive</data>
      <data key="d27">nonlinear</data>
      <data key="d28">moderate (r=0.30-0.45 expected)</data>
      <data key="d29">Countries operating LFG programs demonstrate technical capacity and environmental commitment that correlates with waste management infrastructure. LFG recovery is complementary to material recovery, indicating comprehensive waste management systems.</data>
      <data key="d30">rejected</data>
      <data key="d39">0.07382189377519445</data>
      <data key="d40">0.1277434119880845</data>
      <data key="d41">0.5243242737099074</data>
      <data key="d42">427</data>
      <data key="d35" />
      <data key="d36">log-linear</data>
      <data key="d37" />
      <data key="d38">["Data source not authentic: hypothesis specifies EPA LMOP Landfill Gas Recovery Capacity data, but script uses World Bank renewable electricity capacity (EG.ELC.RNWX.KH) as primary proxy. While data_quality_notes acknowledge this substitution, it represents a material deviation from the specified source.", "Hypothesis interpretation compromised: the proxy variable tested (renewable electricity capacity) is not the same as 'landfill gas recovery capacity.' These are distinct concepts\u2014one is a broad energy infrastructure measure, the other is landfill-specific. The script's own notes state it 'captures broader energy infrastructure' rather than LFG recovery specifically.", "Mechanistic mismatch: the hypothesis's causal mechanism (LFG recovery demonstrates technical capacity and environmental commitment in waste management) is not directly tested by renewable electricity capacity, which may reflect different policy priorities and investment patterns.", "Weak Pearson correlation (r=0.074, p=0.128) does not support the hypothesized positive relationship, though Spearman rho=0.524 is surprisingly strong, suggesting non-linear monotonic structure but high sensitivity to transformations and potential outlier influence.", "Partial correlation was not computed (skipped due to numerical issues), limiting ability to assess confounding by GDP per capita, a critical economic control.", "Log-linear form fits best (R\u00b2=0.201), but linear form explains only 0.48% of variance, raising questions about whether any functional form captures meaningful relationship."]</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:WRR-H10" target="source:us_epa">
      <data key="d23">measured_by</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:WRR-H11" target="indicator:WRR">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">WRR-H11</data>
      <data key="d26">negative</data>
      <data key="d27">threshold</data>
      <data key="d28">strong negative (r=-0.50 to -0.70 expected)</data>
      <data key="d29">Contamination is direct technical barrier to material recovery. Facilities with &gt;10% contamination must halt processing due to safety and economic concerns. Lower contamination allows more waste to be successfully recovered.</data>
      <data key="d30" />
      <data key="d31" />
      <data key="d32" />
      <data key="d33" />
      <data key="d34" />
      <data key="d35" />
      <data key="d36" />
      <data key="d37" />
      <data key="d38">[]</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:WRR-H11" target="source:various_municipal_agencies">
      <data key="d23">measured_by</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:WRR-H12" target="indicator:WRR">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">WRR-H12</data>
      <data key="d26">positive</data>
      <data key="d27">linear</data>
      <data key="d28">moderate to strong (r=0.40-0.60 expected)</data>
      <data key="d29">Source segregation is behavioral prerequisite for all downstream recovery infrastructure. No material recovery facility can recover materials if they arrive pre-mixed. Countries with high household segregation should show elevated WRR due to cleaner material inputs.</data>
      <data key="d30" />
      <data key="d31" />
      <data key="d32" />
      <data key="d33" />
      <data key="d34" />
      <data key="d35" />
      <data key="d36" />
      <data key="d37" />
      <data key="d38">[]</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:WRR-H12" target="source:various_survey_organizations">
      <data key="d23">measured_by</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:WRR-H13" target="indicator:WRR">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">WRR-H13</data>
      <data key="d26">positive</data>
      <data key="d27">log-linear</data>
      <data key="d28">moderate (r=0.35-0.50 expected)</data>
      <data key="d29">Budget allocation indicates institutional commitment and resource availability. Building waste recovery infrastructure requires capital investment of $50-200 million per facility. Countries allocating high per-capita budgets exhibit sustained political commitment.</data>
      <data key="d30" />
      <data key="d31" />
      <data key="d32" />
      <data key="d33" />
      <data key="d34" />
      <data key="d35" />
      <data key="d36" />
      <data key="d37" />
      <data key="d38">[]</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:WRR-H13" target="source:world_bank">
      <data key="d23">measured_by</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:WRR-H14" target="indicator:WRR">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">WRR-H14</data>
      <data key="d26">positive</data>
      <data key="d27">linear</data>
      <data key="d28">strong (r=0.50-0.65 expected)</data>
      <data key="d29">Waste recovery is mechanistically impossible for uncollected waste. Collection coverage is prerequisite for all downstream recovery pathways. High collection rates are necessary though not sufficient for high WRR.</data>
      <data key="d30" />
      <data key="d31" />
      <data key="d32" />
      <data key="d33" />
      <data key="d34" />
      <data key="d35" />
      <data key="d36" />
      <data key="d37" />
      <data key="d38">[]</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:WRR-H14" target="source:world_bank">
      <data key="d23">measured_by</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="proxy:WRR-H15" target="indicator:WRR">
      <data key="d23">proxy_for</data>
      <data key="d24">{}</data>
      <data key="d25">WRR-H15</data>
      <data key="d26">negative</data>
      <data key="d27">nonlinear</data>
      <data key="d28">ambiguous</data>
      <data key="d29">Large informal recycling sectors recover 10-30% of waste streams but indicate poor formal infrastructure. Informal sector size inversely correlates with formal waste management infrastructure, thus predicting low official WRR despite potentially high absolute recovery.</data>
      <data key="d30" />
      <data key="d31" />
      <data key="d32" />
      <data key="d33" />
      <data key="d34" />
      <data key="d35" />
      <data key="d36" />
      <data key="d37" />
